{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "001U", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$x \\times_y z$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0003": "$$\nh_{x \\times_y z}(w) = h_x(w) \\times_{h_y(w)} h_z(w)\n$$", "MATH_0004": "$w$", "MATH_0005": "$x \\times_y z$", "MATH_0006": "$w \\mapsto h_x(w) \\times_{h_y(w)} h_z(w)$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:fae3e9dcd8ee9b7e69766e95004d824a95e8ea4fabd3633cbcb15b811ceb8439", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:6ca81f83f0e665ea577e3d2dc4134cb16ca008dd571f7a37528c5003641da81a", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIf a fibre product exists it is unique up to unique\nisomorphism. This follows from the Yoneda lemma as\nthe definition requires <MATH_0001> to be an object\nof <MATH_0002> such that\n<MATH_0003>\nfunctorially in <MATH_0004>. In other words the fibre product <MATH_0005>\nis an object representing the functor\n<MATH_0006>.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:18842ae39e97552fb680d493f431d6c31c7215324dd66fc6d0bb441713e8142c", "unit_id": "tag:001U:prose-0001"}
